rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在使用 STL 容器的迭代器时，必须确保迭代器不越界。尝试删除超过容器边界或不再有效的迭代器可能导致未定义行为。

######## 规则意义
确保在删除迭代器时不会超过有效的范围，可以避免运行时错误和程序崩溃，提升代码的稳定性和可靠性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    auto it = v.begin();
    ++it; // it 指向 2
    v.erase(it);
    ++it; // it 现在指向 3
    v.erase(it);
    // 此时 it 仍然指向已删除的元素，可能导致越界
    v.erase(it); // 错误：it 越界
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    auto it = v.begin();
    ++it; // it 指向 2
    v.erase(it);
    it = v.begin(); // 重新获取有效的迭代器
    ++it; // it 现在指向 3
    v.erase(it); // 正确：it 不越界
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
When using iterators of STL containers, it is essential to ensure that the iterators do not go out of bounds. Attempting to delete iterators that exceed the container boundaries or are no longer valid may lead to undefined behavior.

######## Rule Significance
Ensuring that iterators do not exceed valid ranges when being deleted can prevent runtime errors and program crashes, enhancing the stability and reliability of the code.

######## Error Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    auto it = v.begin();
    ++it; // it points to 2
    v.erase(it);
    ++it; // it now points to 3
    v.erase(it);
    // At this point, it still points to the deleted element, which may lead to out-of-bounds access
    v.erase(it); // Error: it is out of bounds
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    auto it = v.begin();
    ++it; // it points to 2
    v.erase(it);
    it = v.begin(); // Reacquire a valid iterator
    ++it; // it now points to 3
    v.erase(it); // Correct: it is not out of bounds
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在使用 STL 容器的迭代器时，需确保在对容器进行元素擦除操作之前，迭代器所在的位置是有效的，避免越界。这是防止在运行时引发未定义行为的重要措施。

######## 规则意义
擦除无效的迭代器可能导致程序崩溃或产生异常行为，增加程序不稳定性。确保迭代器的有效性不仅提高了代码的健壮性，还能提高代码的可维护性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> vec = {1, 2, 3};
    std::vector<int>::iterator it = vec.begin();
    vec.erase(it + 5); // 错误: 迭代器越界
    return 0;
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> vec = {1, 2, 3};
    if (vec.size() > 0) {
        std::vector<int>::iterator it = vec.begin();
        vec.erase(it); // 正确: 迭代器是有效的
    }
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
When using iterators of STL containers, it is essential to ensure that the position of the iterator is valid before performing element erase operations on the container to avoid out-of-bounds access. This is an important measure to prevent undefined behavior at runtime.

######## Significance of the Rule
Erasing an invalid iterator may lead to program crashes or abnormal behavior, increasing the instability of the program. Ensuring the validity of the iterator not only enhances the robustness of the code but also improves its maintainability.

######## Error Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> vec = {1, 2, 3};
    std::vector<int>::iterator it = vec.begin();
    vec.erase(it + 5); // Error: Iterator out of bounds
    return 0;
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> vec = {1, 2, 3};
    if (vec.size() > 0) {
        std::vector<int>::iterator it = vec.begin();
        vec.erase(it); // Correct: The iterator is valid
    }
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在使用 `delete` 或 `delete[]` 操作符时，应确保不会抛出异常。任何在析构函数中抛出的异常都可能导致程序未定义的行为。

######## 规则意义
当对象在析构过程中出现异常时，如果未妥善处理，会导致程序崩溃或资源泄露，严重影响程序的稳定性和安全性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() {
        throw std::runtime_error("Destructor exception");
    }
};

void example() {
    MyClass* obj = new MyClass();
    delete obj; // 这里会导致未定义行为
}
``` 

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() noexcept { // 使用 noexcept 确保析构函数不会抛出异常
        // 释放资源
    }
};

void example() {
    MyClass* obj = new MyClass();
    delete obj; // 安全释放
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
When using the `delete` or `delete[]` operator, ensure that no exceptions are thrown. Any exception thrown in a destructor can lead to undefined behavior in the program.

######## Rule Significance
When an exception occurs during the destruction of an object and is not handled properly, it can lead to program crashes or resource leaks, seriously affecting the stability and security of the program.

######## Error Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() {
        throw std::runtime_error("Destructor exception");
    }
};

void example() {
    MyClass* obj = new MyClass();
    delete obj; // This will cause undefined behavior
}
``` 

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() noexcept { // Use noexcept to ensure the destructor does not throw exceptions
        // Release resources
    }
};

void example() {
    MyClass* obj = new MyClass();
    delete obj; // Safe release
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在捕获异常时，如果直接将捕获的异常对象重新抛出，且该对象是通过拷贝产生的，这可能会导致程序行为不如预期，特别是当异常类型包含有状态信息时。

######## 规则意义
此规则的意义在于确保在处理异常时，能够保留异常的原始状态和信息，从而避免因复制异常而导致的信息丢失或不一致。如果需要重新抛出异常，应该直接抛出原始异常，而不是拷贝的异常。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
try {
    // 一些可能抛出异常的代码
} catch (std::exception e) {  
    // 错误：捕获异常后复制并重新抛出
    throw e;  
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
try {
    // 一些可能抛出异常的代码
} catch (const std::exception& e) {  
    // 正确：捕获异常后直接抛出原始异常引用
    throw;  
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
When catching exceptions, rethrowing the caught exception object if it is produced by copying can lead to unexpected program behavior, especially when the exception type contains state information.

######## Rule Significance
The significance of this rule is to ensure that the original state and information of the exception are preserved during exception handling, thus avoiding information loss or inconsistency caused by copying the exception. If it is necessary to rethrow an exception, it should be the original exception rather than a copied one.

######## Error Example
```cpp
try {
    // Some code that may throw an exception
} catch (std::exception e) {  
    // Error: Copying and rethrowing the exception after catching
    throw e;  
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
try {
    // Some code that may throw an exception
} catch (const std::exception& e) {  
    // Correct: Rethrowing the original exception reference after catching
    throw;  
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在C++中，析构函数不应抛出异常。如果析构函数抛出了异常，并且该异常未被捕获，那么将导致程序调用`std::terminate`，这通常会导致程序异常终止。

######## 规则意义
确保析构函数不抛出异常能够提高程序的稳定性和可预测性，同时也使得资源管理更加安全。通过遵循这一规则，可以避免潜在的程序崩溃或不稳定行为，从而提升用户体验。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() {
        throw std::runtime_error("Error in destructor"); // 错误示例
    }
};
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() {
        // 正确示例，不抛出异常
    }
};
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In C++, destructors should not throw exceptions. If a destructor throws an exception and that exception is not caught, it will result in the program calling `std::terminate`, which typically leads to abnormal program termination.

######## Rule Significance
Ensuring that destructors do not throw exceptions enhances the stability and predictability of the program, while also making resource management safer. By adhering to this rule, potential program crashes or unstable behavior can be avoided, thereby improving the user experience.

######## Error Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() {
        throw std::runtime_error("Error in destructor"); // Error example
    }
};
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    ~MyClass() {
        // Correct example, does not throw an exception
    }
};
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在C/C++中，调用`fflush`函数时不应使用输入流（如`stdin`）。该函数应仅用于输出流，使用不当可能导致未定义行为。

######## 规则意义
使用`fflush`来清空输入流可能导致程序异常或不可预期的结果，因此在处理输入流时应避免该操作。

######## 错误示例
'''cpp
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    fflush(stdin); // 错误使用：不应对输入流调用fflush
    return 0;
}
'''

######## 正确示例
'''cpp
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    // 正确做法：仅对输出流调用fflush
    fflush(stdout);
    return 0;
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In C/C++, the `fflush` function should not be used with input streams (such as `stdin`). This function should only be used with output streams, and improper usage may lead to undefined behavior.

######## Significance of the Rule
Using `fflush` to flush input streams may lead to abnormal program behavior or unexpected results, so this operation should be avoided when handling input streams.

######## Incorrect Example
'''cpp
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    fflush(stdin); // Incorrect usage: fflush should not be called on input streams
    return 0;
}
'''

######## Correct Example
'''cpp
#include <cstdio>

int main() {
    // Correct practice: fflush should only be called on output streams
    fflush(stdout);
    return 0;
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
该规则检查浮点数转换过程中可能导致的溢出情况。

######## 规则意义
在将整数或其他类型转换为浮点数时，若待转换的整数超出了浮点数能够表示的最大值，将导致溢出，产生未定义或不正确的结果。这种情况可能导致严重的缺陷，影响程序的稳定性和可靠性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

int main() {
    int largeInt = std::numeric_limits<int>::max();
    float result = largeInt; // 存在浮点数转换溢出 
    std::cout << result << std::endl; // 输出可能不正确 
    return 0;
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

int main() {
    int largeInt = std::numeric_limits<int>::max();
    if (largeInt > std::numeric_limits<float>::max()) {
        std::cerr << "Integer too large for float conversion!" << std::endl;
    } else {
        float result = largeInt;
        std::cout << result << std::endl; 
    }
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule checks for potential overflow situations during the conversion of floating-point numbers.

######## Rule Significance
When converting integers or other types to floating-point numbers, if the integer to be converted exceeds the maximum value that a floating-point number can represent, it will result in overflow, leading to undefined or incorrect results. This situation can cause serious defects, impacting the stability and reliability of the program.

######## Error Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

int main() {
    int largeInt = std::numeric_limits<int>::max();
    float result = largeInt; // Floating-point conversion overflow
    std::cout << result << std::endl; // Output may be incorrect
    return 0;
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

int main() {
    int largeInt = std::numeric_limits<int>::max();
    if (largeInt > std::numeric_limits<float>::max()) {
        std::cerr << "Integer too large for float conversion!" << std::endl;
    } else {
        float result = largeInt;
        std::cout << result << std::endl; 
    }
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content:
