rules.content:
####### 规则概述
本规则旨在提醒开发者在访问数组或集合时，注意使用对象索引。错误的索引访问可能导致未定义行为或访问非法内存。

######## 规则意义
确保使用有效的索引有助于提高代码的安全性和稳定性。开发者应当避免越界访问，确保在使用索引时始终处于有效范围内。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 3};
    // 错误: 访问越界
    std::cout << numbers[3] << std::endl; // 未定义行为
    return 0;
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 3};
    // 正确: 索引在范围内
    if (numbers.size() > 3) {
        std::cout << numbers[3] << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Index out of range" << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule aims to remind developers to pay attention to using object indices when accessing arrays or collections. Incorrect index access may lead to undefined behavior or access to illegal memory.

######## Rule Significance
Ensuring the use of valid indices helps to improve the security and stability of the code. Developers should avoid out-of-bounds access and ensure they are always within a valid range when using indices.

######## Error Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 3};
    // Error: Out of bounds access
    std::cout << numbers[3] << std::endl; // Undefined behavior
    return 0;
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

int main() {
    std::vector<int> numbers = {1, 2, 3};
    // Correct: Index is within range
    if (numbers.size() > 3) {
        std::cout << numbers[3] << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "Index out of range" << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在使用操作符'='时，缺少返回语句将导致函数未能返回预期的值。这可能导致意外行为和难以检测的错误。

######## 规则意义
确保在实现自定义的操作符重载时，必须显式返回一个值。缺失返回语句可能会导致应用程序在运行时崩溃，或者产生未定义的行为。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    bool operator=(const MyClass& other) {
        // 缺失返回语句
        // ... 执行一些操作
    }
};
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    bool operator=(const MyClass& other) {
        // 执行一些操作
        return true; // 必须返回一个值
    }
};
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
When using the operator '=', the absence of a return statement will cause the function to fail to return the expected value. This may lead to unexpected behavior and hard-to-detect errors.

######## Rule Significance
It is essential to explicitly return a value when implementing custom operator overloading. Missing a return statement may cause the application to crash at runtime or result in undefined behavior.

######## Error Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    bool operator=(const MyClass& other) {
        // Missing return statement
        // ... perform some operations
    }
};
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    bool operator=(const MyClass& other) {
        // Perform some operations
        return true; // Must return a value
    }
};
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在C++中，使用运算符重载返回自身引用是一个常见的做法。然而，如果类的设计不合理，可能导致意外的逻辑错误。

######## 规则意义
当运算符重载返回自身引用时，开发者需要确保该操作是安全的，避免产生悬空引用或不必要的副作用。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class InvalidOperator {
public:
    InvalidOperator& operator=(const InvalidOperator& other) {
        // 忽略自我赋值检查，可能导致逻辑错误
        this->value = other.value;
        return *this;
    }
private:
    int value;
};
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class ValidOperator {
public:
    ValidOperator& operator=(const ValidOperator& other) {
        if (this != &other) { // 自我赋值检查
            this->value = other.value;
        }
        return *this;
    }
private:
    int value;
};
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In C++, returning a reference to itself in operator overloading is a common practice. However, if a class is poorly designed, it may lead to unexpected logical errors.

######## Rule Meaning
When an operator overload returns a reference to itself, developers need to ensure that the operation is safe to avoid dangling references or unnecessary side effects.

######## Incorrect Example
```cpp
class InvalidOperator {
public:
    InvalidOperator& operator=(const InvalidOperator& other) {
        // Ignoring self-assignment check, which may lead to logical errors
        this->value = other.value;
        return *this;
    }
private:
    int value;
};
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class ValidOperator {
public:
    ValidOperator& operator=(const ValidOperator& other) {
        if (this != &other) { // Self-assignment check
            this->value = other.value;
        }
        return *this;
    }
private:
    int value;
};
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在类中，如果重载了运算符`==`（等于）但是没有实现此运算符，应该留空未实现，而不是让其返回默认的`false`。

######## 规则意义
实现运算符`==`时，应该确保该运算符有明确且合理的实现。如果一个类的运算符`==`没有实现，却意外返回`false`，可能会导致逻辑错误，影响程序行为。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    bool operator==(const MyClass&) const;
};

// 这里没有实现 operator==，会隐含返回 false
MyClass a, b;
if (a == b) { /* 逻辑错误 */ }
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    bool operator==(const MyClass&) const {
        // 实现逻辑
        return true; // 示例实现
    }
};

MyClass a, b;
if (a == b) { /* 正确逻辑 */ }
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In a class, if the operator `==` (equal to) is overloaded but not implemented, it should be left unimplemented rather than allowing it to return the default `false`.

######## Rule Significance
When implementing the operator `==`, it is essential to ensure that the operator has a clear and reasonable implementation. If a class's operator `==` is not implemented and inadvertently returns `false`, it may lead to logical errors and affect the program's behavior.

######## Error Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    bool operator==(const MyClass&) const;
};

// The operator== is not implemented here, which will implicitly return false
MyClass a, b;
if (a == b) { /* Logic Error */ }
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    bool operator==(const MyClass&) const {
        // Implementation logic
        return true; // Example implementation
    }
};

MyClass a, b;
if (a == b) { /* Correct Logic */ }
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
此规则检查在赋值操作中，使用了错误的类型或不恰当的运算符导致的错误。

######## 规则意义
使用不正确的运算符进行变量赋值可能导致逻辑错误，进而引发程序崩溃或严重的安全漏洞。因此，在代码中确保运算符的正确性是极为重要的。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
int a = 5;
if (a = 10) {  // 错误：使用了赋值运算符=而非比较运算符==
    // do something
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
int a = 5;
if (a == 10) {  // 正确：使用了比较运算符==
    // do something
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule checks for errors caused by using incorrect types or inappropriate operators in assignment operations.

######## Rule Meaning
Using incorrect operators for variable assignment can lead to logical errors, which may result in program crashes or serious security vulnerabilities. Therefore, ensuring the correctness of operators in the code is extremely important.

######## Error Example
```cpp
int a = 5;
if (a = 10) {  // Error: used assignment operator = rather than comparison operator ==
    // do something
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
int a = 5;
if (a == 10) {  // Correct: used comparison operator ==
    // do something
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
该规则检测代码中是否存在对立的布尔表达式，例如在条件判断中同时使用了正向与反向的判断，可能导致逻辑错误。

######## 规则意义
使用对立表达式可能导致代码逻辑混乱，降低可读性，并可能引入潜在隐患，导致未预期的行为。此类错误如果没有被及时发现，会对程序的执行结果产生重大影响。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
bool a = true;
if (a || !a) {
    // 逻辑错误
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
bool a = true;
if (a) {
    // 正确的逻辑处理
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule detects the presence of opposing Boolean expressions in the code, such as using both positive and negative conditions in a conditional statement, which may lead to logical errors.

######## Rule Significance
Using opposing expressions can lead to confusing logic in the code, reduce readability, and potentially introduce risks, leading to unexpected behavior. Such errors, if not discovered in a timely manner, can have a significant impact on the execution results of the program.

######## Error Example
```cpp
bool a = true;
if (a || !a) {
    // Logical error
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
bool a = true;
if (a) {
    // Correct logical handling
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在条件语句中，如果存在对立的条件，则可能导致逻辑错误和不可预测的行为。

######## 规则意义
对立的条件会导致代码逻辑混乱，难以维护和理解。这种情况通常是一个潜在的缺陷，可能会导致未定义的行为或异常。识别并修改这些对立的条件可以提高代码的可读性和可靠性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
if (x > 0) {
    // do something
} else if (x < 0) {
    // do something different
} else {
    // 访问对立条件
    // 这部分逻辑可能会导致逻辑错误
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
if (x > 0) {
    // do something
} else if (x < 0) {
    // do something different
} else {
    // x == 0 情况
    // 处理此条件
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In conditional statements, the presence of conflicting conditions may lead to logical errors and unpredictable behavior.

######## Rule Significance
Conflicting conditions can cause confusion in code logic, making it difficult to maintain and understand. This situation is often a potential defect that can lead to undefined behavior or exceptions. Identifying and modifying these conflicting conditions can improve the readability and reliability of the code.

######## Error Example
```cpp
if (x > 0) {
    // do something
} else if (x < 0) {
    // do something different
} else {
    // Accessing conflicting conditions
    // This part of the logic may lead to logical errors
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
if (x > 0) {
    // do something
} else if (x < 0) {
    // do something different
} else {
    // x == 0 case
    // Handle this condition
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
此规则用于检测在字符串比较时，传递给函数的字符串参数是否可能重叠。这种重叠可能导致未定义的行为。

######## 规则意义
使用重叠字符串调用比较函数可能导致程序出现异常。在某些情况下，可能会导致应用崩溃、数据损坏或安全漏洞。因此，确保在进行字符串比较时，避免使用重叠的字符串区域是非常重要的。

######## 错误示例
'''cpp
#include <cstring>

void compareStrings(char* a, char* b) {
    if (strcmp(a, b) == 0) {
        // Do something
    }
}

int main() {
    char str[] = "example";
    // 传递重叠的区域
    compareStrings(str, str + 3);
    return 0;
}
'''

######## 正确示例
'''cpp
#include <cstring>

void compareStrings(const char* a, const char* b) {
    if (strcmp(a, b) == 0) {
        // Do something
    }
}

int main() {
    const char* str1 = "example";
    const char* str2 = "test";
    compareStrings(str1, str2);
    return 0;
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule is used to detect whether the string parameters passed to a function during string comparisons may overlap. Such overlaps can lead to undefined behavior.

######## Significance of the Rule
Calling comparison functions with overlapping strings may result in abnormal program behavior. In certain cases, this may lead to application crashes, data corruption, or security vulnerabilities. Therefore, it is crucial to ensure that overlapping string areas are avoided during string comparisons.

######## Error Example
'''cpp
#include <cstring>

void compareStrings(char* a, char* b) {
    if (strcmp(a, b) == 0) {
        // Do something
    }
}

int main() {
    char str[] = "example";
    // Passing overlapping regions
    compareStrings(str, str + 3);
    return 0;
}
'''

######## Correct Example
'''cpp
#include <cstring>

void compareStrings(const char* a, const char* b) {
    if (strcmp(a, b) == 0) {
        // Do something
    }
}

int main() {
    const char* str1 = "example";
    const char* str2 = "test";
    compareStrings(str1, str2);
    return 0;
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在同一内存区域进行重叠写入可能导致数据损坏或未定义行为。该规则关注函数调用中可能出现的重叠写入问题。

######## 规则意义
识别和避免重叠写入可以提高代码的安全性和稳定性，减少潜在的缺陷和不必要的调试开销。

######## 错误示例
'''cpp
#include <cstring>

void example() {
    char buffer[10];
    strcpy(buffer, "hello"); // 合法写入
    strncpy(buffer + 2, "world", 5); // 重叠写入，可能导致未定义行为
}
'''

######## 正确示例
'''cpp
#include <cstring>

void example() {
    char buffer[10];
    strcpy(buffer, "hello"); // 合法写入
    char temp[10];
    strncpy(temp, "world", 5); // 避免重叠，使用临时缓冲区
    // 可在此进一步处理temp
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Overview of the Rule
Overlapping writes in the same memory area can lead to data corruption or undefined behavior. This rule focuses on the potential issues of overlapping writes that may occur during function calls.

######## Significance of the Rule
Identifying and avoiding overlapping writes can enhance the safety and stability of the code, reducing potential defects and unnecessary debugging overhead.

######## Error Example
'''cpp
#include <cstring>

void example() {
    char buffer[10];
    strcpy(buffer, "hello"); // Valid write
    strncpy(buffer + 2, "world", 5); // Overlapping write, may cause undefined behavior
}
'''

######## Correct Example
'''cpp
#include <cstring>

void example() {
    char buffer[10];
    strcpy(buffer, "hello"); // Valid write
    char temp[10];
    strncpy(temp, "world", 5); // Avoid overlap, use a temporary buffer
    // Further processing of temp can be done here
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
该规则检测到联合体（union）中的重叠写入，可能导致未定义行为。

######## 规则意义
在C/C++中，联合体的成员共享同一段内存，如果对一个成员的写入与其他成员重叠，读取这些成员时的结果可能不可预测，会引发难以发现的问题。

######## 错误示例
'''cpp
union Data {
    int i;
    float f;
};

void example() {
    Data data;
    data.i = 5; // 写入整数
    data.f = 3.14f; // 重叠写入浮点数
    // 此时，data.i和data.f的值可能是不确定的
}
''' 

######## 正确示例
'''cpp
union Data {
    int i;
    float f;
};

void example() {
    Data data;
    data.i = 5; // 写入整数
    // 读取data.i 
    int value = data.i;
    // 如果需要使用浮点数，建议重置内存
data.f = 3.14f; // 安全地重新写入
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule detects overlapping writes in a union, which may lead to undefined behavior.

######## Rule Meaning
In C/C++, the members of a union share the same memory location. If a write to one member overlaps with other members, the results of reading those members may be unpredictable, leading to hard-to-detect issues.

 ######## Error Example
'''cpp
union Data {
    int i;
    float f;
};

void example() {
    Data data;
    data.i = 5; // Write integer
    data.f = 3.14f; // Overlapping write of float
    // At this point, the values of data.i and data.f may be indeterminate.
}
''' 

######## Correct Example
'''cpp
union Data {
    int i;
    float f;
};

void example() {
    Data data;
    data.i = 5; // Write integer
    // Read data.i 
    int value = data.i;
    // If a float is needed, it is recommended to reset the memory
data.f = 3.14f; // Safely rewrite
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在C/C++中，按值传递会导致数据的拷贝，可能会影响性能，尤其是在处理大型对象时。

######## 规则意义
对于大对象，按值传递可能导致不必要的性能损失，应当使用按引用传递以提高程序的效率。

######## 错误示例
'''cpp
void processData(std::vector<int> data) {
    // 一些处理逻辑
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> largeData(100000);
    processData(largeData); // 按值传递，会拷贝大量数据
}
'''

######## 正确示例
'''cpp
void processData(const std::vector<int>& data) {
    // 一些处理逻辑
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> largeData(100000);
    processData(largeData); // 按引用传递，无数据拷贝
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In C/C++, passing by value results in data copying, which may impact performance, especially when dealing with large objects.

######## Rule Significance
For large objects, passing by value can lead to unnecessary performance losses; passing by reference should be used to improve the efficiency of the program.

######## Incorrect Example
'''cpp
void processData(std::vector<int> data) {
    // Some processing logic
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> largeData(100000);
    processData(largeData); // Passing by value, large data is copied
}
'''

######## Correct Example
'''cpp
void processData(const std::vector<int>& data) {
    // Some processing logic
}

int main() {
    std::vector<int> largeData(100000);
    processData(largeData); // Passing by reference, no data copy
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在对指针进行加法操作后，结果不应为NULL。指针加法的结果代表了内存地址的偏移，如果结果为NULL，这通常意味着访问了一个无效的内存地址，可能导致程序崩溃或未定义行为。

######## 规则意义
确保在进行指针加法操作时，程序能够正确处理结果，避免潜在的内存访问错误和程序崩溃。这有助于提高代码的稳定性和安全性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
int array[10];
int* ptr = array;
ptr += 10; // ptr 指向 array 之后的位置，可能导致未定义行为。
if (ptr != nullptr) { // 此处 ptr 永远不等于 nullptr
    // 访问 ptr 可能导致程序崩溃
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
int array[10];
int* ptr = array;
