rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
After performing addition operations on pointers, the result should not be NULL. The result of pointer addition represents an offset in memory addresses; if the result is NULL, it usually means that an invalid memory address has been accessed, which may lead to program crashes or undefined behavior.

######## Rule Significance
Ensure that the program correctly handles the result when performing pointer addition operations to avoid potential memory access errors and program crashes. This helps improve the stability and safety of the code.

######## Error Example
```cpp
int array[10];
int* ptr = array;
ptr += 10; // ptr points to a position after array, which may lead to undefined behavior.
if (ptr != nullptr) { // ptr will never be equal to nullptr here
    // Accessing ptr may cause the program to crash
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
int array[10];
int* ptr = array;
if (ptr + 1 < array + 10) { // Check if the pointer is within a valid range
    ptr += 1; // Now ptr points to the second element
}
```

