rules.content:
####### 规则概述
该规则用于检查代码中存在的冗余条件，这些条件不会影响程序的逻辑和执行结果。

######## 规则意义
冗余条件不仅会使代码变得复杂且难以维护，还可能导致程序的性能下降。因此，消除这些冗余条件可以提高代码的可读性和效率。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
if (x > 0 && x > 1) {
    // do something
}
```
该示例中 `x > 0` 的条件是冗余的，因为如果 `x > 1` 为真，那么 `x > 0` 一定也为真。

######## 正确示例
```cpp
if (x > 1) {
    // do something
}
```
在该示例中，我们仅保留了必要的条件，使得代码更加简洁和清晰。

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule is used to check for redundant conditions in the code that do not affect the logic and execution results of the program.

######## Rule Meaning
Redundant conditions not only make the code complex and difficult to maintain, but they can also lead to performance degradation of the program. Therefore, eliminating these redundant conditions can improve the readability and efficiency of the code.

######## Error Example
```cpp
if (x > 0 && x > 1) {
    // do something
}
```
In this example, the condition `x > 0` is redundant because if `x > 1` is true, then `x > 0` must also be true.

######## Correct Example
```cpp
if (x > 1) {
    // do something
}
```
In this example, we have retained only the necessary condition, making the code more concise and clear.

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在C/C++中，冗余拷贝指的是不必要的对象拷贝，这可能导致性能下降，尤其是在拷贝大型对象时。

######## 规则意义
避免冗余拷贝可以提高程序的效率，减少不必要的资源消耗，尤其是在高性能应用中，需尽量减少开销。

######## 错误示例
'''cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass(int value) : value(value) {}
    // 拷贝构造函数
    MyClass(const MyClass& other) : value(other.value) {}
private:
    int value;
};

void function() {
    MyClass obj1(10);
    MyClass obj2 = obj1;  // 这里进行了一次冗余拷贝
}
'''  

######## 正确示例
'''cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass(int value) : value(value) {}
    MyClass(const MyClass& other) = default;  // 使用默认拷贝构造函数
private:
    int value;
};

void function() {
    MyClass obj1(10);
    MyClass obj2 = std::move(obj1);  // 使用移动语义，而非冗余拷贝
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In C/C++, redundant copy refers to unnecessary object copies, which can lead to performance degradation, especially when copying large objects.

######## Rule Meaning
Avoiding redundant copies can enhance program efficiency and reduce unnecessary resource consumption, particularly in high-performance applications, where minimizing overhead is crucial.

######## Incorrect Example
'''cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass(int value) : value(value) {}
    // Copy constructor
    MyClass(const MyClass& other) : value(other.value) {}
private:
    int value;
};

void function() {
    MyClass obj1(10);
    MyClass obj2 = obj1;  // A redundant copy is made here
}
'''  

######## Correct Example
'''cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass(int value) : value(value) {}
    MyClass(const MyClass& other) = default;  // Use default copy constructor
private:
    int value;
};

void function() {
    MyClass obj1(10);
    MyClass obj2 = std::move(obj1);  // Use move semantics instead of redundant copy
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
此规则检测到在作用域中创建了一个不必要的局部常量拷贝。这会导致不必要的内存开销。

######## 规则意义
通过消除冗余的局部常量拷贝，可以提高代码的性能和可读性，减少内存开销，从而提高程序的整体效率。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
void exampleFunction(const std::string &input) {
    std::string copy = input; // 冗余的拷贝
    // 使用copy的代码...
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
void exampleFunction(const std::string &input) {
    const std::string &ref = input; // 使用引用而不是拷贝
    // 使用ref的代码...
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule detects the creation of an unnecessary local constant copy within the scope. This can lead to unnecessary memory overhead.

######## Rule Significance
By eliminating redundant local constant copies, code performance and readability can be improved, memory overhead reduced, and the overall efficiency of the program enhanced.

######## Error Example
```cpp
void exampleFunction(const std::string &input) {
    std::string copy = input; // Redundant copy
    // Code using copy...
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
void exampleFunction(const std::string &input) {
    const std::string &ref = input; // Use reference instead of copy
    // Code using ref...
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
此规则旨在检测在使用指针时存在的冗余操作，这类操作可能导致代码的可读性降低，并增加理解代码的难度。

######## 规则意义
避免冗余的指针操作有助于提高代码的简洁性和可维护性，确保开发人员在阅读和理解代码时能够更迅速地抓住重点。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
int* p = new int(5);
int** pp = &p; // 不必要的冗余指针操作
delete p;
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
int* p = new int(5);
// 直接使用指针p，而不使用冗余的pp
delete p;
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule aims to detect redundant operations that occur when using pointers, which may reduce code readability and increase the difficulty of understanding the code.

######## Rule Significance
Avoiding redundant pointer operations helps to improve the conciseness and maintainability of the code, ensuring that developers can quickly grasp the key points when reading and understanding the code.

######## Error Example
```cpp
int* p = new int(5);
int** pp = &p; // Unnecessary redundant pointer operation
delete p;
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
int* p = new int(5);
// Directly use pointer p without the redundant pp
delete p;
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
资源泄漏发生在程序未能正确释放动态分配的内存或系统资源的情况下。这可能导致应用程序消耗越来越多的内存，从而导致性能下降，甚至崩溃。

######## 规则意义
发现并修复资源泄漏可以提高程序的稳定性和性能，避免由于资源耗尽引发的潜在问题，确保系统的可靠运行。

######## 错误示例
'''cpp
void example() {
    int* ptr = new int[10];
    // 忘记释放内存，造成资源泄漏
}
'''

######## 正确示例
'''cpp
void example() {
    int* ptr = new int[10];
    // 正确释放内存
    delete[] ptr;
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
Resource leaks occur when a program fails to properly release dynamically allocated memory or system resources. This can lead to increased memory consumption by the application, resulting in performance degradation or even crashes.

######## Significance of the Rule
Detecting and fixing resource leaks can enhance the stability and performance of the program, avoiding potential issues caused by resource exhaustion and ensuring reliable operation of the system.

######## Error Example
'''cpp
void example() {
    int* ptr = new int[10];
    // Forgetting to release memory, causing a resource leak
}
'''

######## Correct Example
'''cpp
void example() {
    int* ptr = new int[10];
    // Correctly releasing memory
    delete[] ptr;
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在C++中，重抛异常时应确保当前正在处理的异常被捕获。如果没有捕获当前异常，而直接重抛其他异常，可能导致程序的异常处理机制不完善，导致潜在的错误。

######## 规则意义
捕获并处理当前异常是确保程序正常运行的关键。如果在没有捕获当前异常的情况下重抛，那么原有的异常信息可能会丢失，造成后续的错误定位和处理困难。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
try {
    throw std::runtime_error("Original exception");
} catch (const std::exception&) {
    throw std::logic_error("New exception"); // 未捕获当前异常
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
try {
    throw std::runtime_error("Original exception");
} catch (const std::exception& e) {
    // 处理当前异常
    std::cerr << "Caught: " << e.what() << '\n';
    throw; // 重抛当前异常
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In C++, when rethrowing an exception, it is essential to ensure that the currently handled exception is caught. If the current exception is not caught and another exception is directly rethrown, it may lead to an incomplete exception handling mechanism in the program, resulting in potential errors.

######## Rule Significance
Capturing and handling the current exception is key to ensuring the normal operation of the program. If the current exception is rethrown without being caught, the original exception information may be lost, making subsequent error location and handling difficult.

######## Error Example
```cpp
try {
    throw std::runtime_error("Original exception");
} catch (const std::exception&) {
    throw std::logic_error("New exception"); // Current exception not caught
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
try {
    throw std::runtime_error("Original exception");
} catch (const std::exception& e) {
    // Handle the current exception
    std::cerr << "Caught: " << e.what() << '\n';
    throw; // Rethrow the current exception
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在函数中不应该通过引用返回局部变量的值，因为局部变量在函数结束后会被销毁，导致返回的引用指向无效内存。

######## 规则意义
返回局部变量的引用可能导致未定义行为，可能会导致程序崩溃或者数据损坏。因此，应避免此类用法，以提高代码的安全性和稳定性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
int& erroneousFunction() {
    int localVar = 42;
    return localVar; // 这里返回一个局部变量的引用
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
int correctFunction() {
    int localVar = 42;
    return localVar; // 返回值而不是引用
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
You should not return the value of a local variable by reference from a function, because local variables are destroyed after the function ends, leading to the returned reference pointing to invalid memory.

######## Rule Significance
Returning a reference to a local variable can lead to undefined behavior, which may cause program crashes or data corruption. Therefore, such usage should be avoided to enhance the safety and stability of the code.

######## Wrong Example
```cpp
int& erroneousFunction() {
    int localVar = 42;
    return localVar; // This returns a reference to a local variable
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
int correctFunction() {
    int localVar = 42;
    return localVar; // Returns the value instead of a reference
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
此规则检测是否存在返回悬空指针的情况，即返回的指针在其引用的对象的生命周期结束后仍然可以被访问。

######## 规则意义
返回悬空指针可能导致未定义行为、程序崩溃或数据损坏等问题。确保返回的指针在使用期间引用的对象仍然有效，能有效避免这些潜在问题。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() { data = new int(10); }
    ~MyClass() { delete data; }
    int* getData() { return data; }
private:
    int* data;
};

void test() {
    MyClass obj;
    int* p = obj.getData(); // 这里返回的数据指针在 obj 的生命周期结束后将变为悬空
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() { data = new int(10); }
    ~MyClass() { delete data; }
    int getData() { return *data; } // 返回数据值而不是指针
private:
    int* data;
};

void test() {
    MyClass obj;
    int value = obj.getData(); // 这里获取的是值，不会有悬空指针问题
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule detects instances where dangling pointers are returned, meaning that the returned pointer can still be accessed after the lifecycle of the object it references has ended.

######## Rule Meaning
Returning a dangling pointer can lead to undefined behavior, program crashes, or data corruption. Ensuring that the returned pointer references an object that is still valid during its usage effectively avoids these potential issues.

######## Error Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() { data = new int(10); }
    ~MyClass() { delete data; }
    int* getData() { return data; }
private:
    int* data;
};

void test() {
    MyClass obj;
    int* p = obj.getData(); // The returned data pointer will become dangling after obj's lifecycle ends
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class MyClass {
public:
    MyClass() { data = new int(10); }
    ~MyClass() { delete data; }
    int getData() { return *data; } // Returns the data value instead of the pointer
private:
    int* data;
};

void test() {
    MyClass obj;
    int value = obj.getData(); // Here, the value is retrieved, and there won't be a dangling pointer issue
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
该规则旨在防止在函数中返回对局部变量的引用，这可能导致未定义行为或潜在的程序崩溃。

######## 规则意义
返回对局部变量的引用是一个常见的错误，它使得引用变得无效，因为局部变量在函数返回后会被销毁。这会导致调用者使用过期的引用，从而可能引发错误和程序崩溃。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
int& getValue() {
    int a = 5;
    return a; // 错误：返回局部变量的引用
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
int getValue() {
    int a = 5;
    return a; // 正确：返回值而非引用
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule is designed to prevent returning references to local variables from functions, which can lead to undefined behavior or potential program crashes.

######## Rule Significance
Returning references to local variables is a common mistake that invalidates the reference since local variables are destroyed after the function returns. This can lead to the caller using a stale reference, potentially causing errors and program crashes.

######## Error Example
```cpp
int& getValue() {
    int a = 5;
    return a; // Error: returning a reference to a local variable
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
int getValue() {
    int a = 5;
    return a; // Correct: returning a value instead of a reference
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在C++中，使用`std::move`来返回局部变量是不安全的，因为局部变量在函数返回时可能会被销毁，这可能导致未定义行为。

######## 规则意义
避免返回局部变量的`std::move`值可以防止潜在的错误或未定义行为，确保程序的稳定性和可预测性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
int* getPointer() {
    int localValue = 42;
    return std::move(&localValue); // 错误: 返回局部变量的指针
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
int getValue() {
    int localValue = 42;
    return localValue; // 合法: 直接返回值
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
In C++, using `std::move` to return a local variable is unsafe because local variables may be destroyed when the function returns, which can lead to undefined behavior.

######## Rule Significance
Avoiding the return of `std::move` values of local variables can prevent potential errors or undefined behavior, ensuring the stability and predictability of the program.

######## Erroneous Example
```cpp
int* getPointer() {
    int localValue = 42;
    return std::move(&localValue); // Error: Returning a pointer to a local variable
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
int getValue() {
    int localValue = 42;
    return localValue; // Legal: Returning the value directly
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
返回临时对象的引用可能导致未定义的行为，建议避免这种做法。

######## 规则意义
返回临时对象的引用会使得调用者在使用该引用时，访问到已经被销毁的对象，从而引发未定义的行为。此规则旨在确保代码的安全性与稳定性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class Example {
public:
    Example() {}
    // 返回临时对象的引用
    Example& get() {
        return Example(); // 错误，返回已经销毁的对象引用
    }
};

void test() {
    Example& obj = Example().get(); // obj 引用的是已销毁的对象
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class Example {
public:
    Example() {}
    // 返回对象的副本而不是引用
    Example get() {
        return Example(); // 正确，返回对象的副本
    }
};

void test() {
    Example obj = Example().get(); // obj 正常使用
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
Returning a reference to a temporary object may lead to undefined behavior, and such practice is discouraged.

######## Rule Significance
Returning a reference to a temporary object allows the caller to access an object that has already been destroyed, which can result in undefined behavior. This rule aims to ensure the safety and stability of the code.

######## Error Example
```cpp
class Example {
public:
    Example() {}
    // Returning a reference to a temporary object
    Example& get() {
        return Example(); // Error, returning a reference to a destroyed object
    }
};

void test() {
    Example& obj = Example().get(); // obj references a destroyed object
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class Example {
public:
    Example() {}
    // Returning a copy of the object instead of a reference
    Example get() {
        return Example(); // Correct, returning a copy of the object
    }
};

void test() {
    Example obj = Example().get(); // obj is used normally
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在遍历容器的过程中，使用了相同的迭代器表达式，可能会导致逻辑错误。

######## 规则意义
使用相同的迭代器表达式可能会导致代码的可读性下降，也可能会产生意想不到的逻辑错误，尤其是在规模较大的项目中。在同一循环或相似的循环中使用不恰当的迭代器可能会引起混淆和潜在的缺陷。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

void example(std::vector<int>& vec) {
    for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); ++it) {
        // 进行一些处理
        for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); ++it) {
            // 重复使用相同的迭代器，可能会导致逻辑错误
            std::cout << *it << " ";
        }
    }
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

void example(std::vector<int>& vec) {
    for (auto it1 = vec.begin(); it1 != vec.end(); ++it1) {
        // 进行一些处理
        for (auto it2 = vec.begin(); it2 != vec.end(); ++it2) {
            // 使用不同的迭代器，避免逻辑错误
            std::cout << *it2 << " ";
        }
    }
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
Using the same iterator expression during the traversal of a container may lead to logical errors.

######## Rule Meaning
Using the same iterator expression may decrease the readability of the code and could introduce unexpected logical errors, especially in larger projects. Using inappropriate iterators in the same loop or similar loops may cause confusion and potential defects.

######## Incorrect Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

void example(std::vector<int>& vec) {
    for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); ++it) {
        // Perform some processing
        for (auto it = vec.begin(); it != vec.end(); ++it) {
            // Reusing the same iterator may lead to logical errors
            std::cout << *it << " ";
        }
    }
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <vector>
#include <iostream>

void example(std::vector<int>& vec) {
    for (auto it1 = vec.begin(); it1 != vec.end(); ++it1) {
        // Perform some processing
        for (auto it2 = vec.begin(); it2 != vec.end(); ++it2) {
            // Use different iterators to avoid logical errors
            std::cout << *it2 << " ";
        }
    }
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
该规则检测在对文件进行追加操作时，可能导致文件指针位置错误的情况。

######## 规则意义
在追加数据到文件时，如果没有正确管理文件指针的位置，可能会导致数据被写入错误的位置，甚至覆盖文件中已有数据。这类问题可能导致数据丢失或不一致性。

######## 错误示例
'''cpp
#include <fstream>

void appendData(const std::string& filename) {
    std::ofstream file(filename, std::ios::app);
    file << "Initial data\n";
    // 忽视了文件指针状态，直接对文件进行再写入操作
    file << "Additional data\n";
    file.close();
}
'''

######## 正确示例
'''cpp
#include <fstream>

void appendData(const std::string& filename) {
    std::ofstream file(filename, std::ios::app);
    file << "Initial data\n";
    // 在添加数据之前，确保文件指针在文件末尾
    file.seekp(0, std::ios::end);
    file << "Additional data\n";
    file.close();
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule detects situations that may lead to incorrect file pointer positions when appending to a file.

######## Rule Significance
When appending data to a file, not managing the file pointer position correctly may cause data to be written in the wrong location, potentially overwriting existing data in the file. Such issues could result in data loss or inconsistency.

######## Error Example
'''cpp
#include <fstream>

void appendData(const std::string& filename) {
    std::ofstream file(filename, std::ios::app);
    file << "Initial data\n";
    // Ignored the file pointer state and directly wrote to the file again
    file << "Additional data\n";
    file.close();
}
'''

######## Correct Example
'''cpp
#include <fstream>

void appendData(const std::string& filename) {
    std::ofstream file(filename, std::ios::app);
    file << "Initial data\n";
    // Ensure the file pointer is at the end of the file before adding data
    file.seekp(0, std::ios::end);
    file << "Additional data\n";
    file.close();
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
自我赋值指的是对象在赋值操作中将自身的值赋给自己，这种情况通常会引发不必要的开销，甚至可能导致不期望的行为。

######## 规则意义
自我赋值在代码上通常是一个潜在的错误，它可能导致资源的冗余分配或逻辑上的混淆。因此，避免自我赋值可以提高代码的可读性和可维护性。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class Example {
public:
    Example& operator=(const Example& other) {
        // 错误：处理自我赋值的情况
        if (this == &other) {
            return *this;
        }
        // 其他赋值逻辑
        return *this;
    }
};
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class Example {
public:
    Example& operator=(const Example& other) {
        // 正确处理自我赋值的情况
        if (this != &other) {
            // 其他赋值逻辑
        }
        return *this;
    }
};
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
Self-assignment refers to the situation where an object assigns its own value to itself during an assignment operation. This scenario can often lead to unnecessary overhead and might even result in unexpected behavior.

######## Rule Significance
Self-assignment is generally a potential error in code, as it may cause redundant resource allocation or logical confusion. Therefore, avoiding self-assignment can enhance the readability and maintainability of the code.

######## Incorrect Example
```cpp
class Example {
public:
    Example& operator=(const Example& other) {
        // Error: Handling self-assignment case
        if (this == &other) {
            return *this;
        }
        // Other assignment logic
        return *this;
    }
};
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class Example {
public:
    Example& operator=(const Example& other) {
        // Correctly handling self-assignment case
        if (this != &other) {
            // Other assignment logic
        }
        return *this;
    }
};
```

