rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在使用可变参数宏`va_start`、`va_arg`和`va_end`时，确保在每次调用中正确定义和处理参数，以避免内存错误和未定义行为。

######## 规则意义
在C/C++中，可变参数函数允许传递不定数量的参数，但是错误的使用`va_start`和相应的参数处理可能导致内存错误、程序崩溃或数据不一致。遵循正确的顺序和数量的处理非常重要。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <cstdarg>
#include <cstdio>

void myFunction(int count, ...){
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, count);
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) { // 错误：假定输入为5个参数
        int value = va_arg(args, int); // 可能读取到未定义的参数
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    va_end(args);
}

int main() {
    myFunction(3, 1, 2, 3);
    return 0;
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <cstdarg>
#include <cstdio>

void myFunction(int count, ...){
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, count);
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) { // 正确：根据实际参数数量来处理
        int value = va_arg(args, int);
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    va_end(args);
}

int main() {
    myFunction(3, 1, 2, 3); // 正确调用
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
When using variable argument macros `va_start`, `va_arg`, and `va_end`, ensure that parameters are correctly defined and handled in each call to avoid memory errors and undefined behavior.

######## Rule Significance
In C/C++, variable argument functions allow the passing of an indefinite number of parameters; however, incorrect usage of `va_start` and the corresponding parameter handling can lead to memory errors, program crashes, or data inconsistencies. It is crucial to follow the correct order and number of arguments handling.

######## Error Example
```cpp
#include <cstdarg>
#include <cstdio>

void myFunction(int count, ...){
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, count);
    for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) { // Error: assuming input to be 5 parameters
        int value = va_arg(args, int); // May read undefined parameters
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    va_end(args);
}

int main() {
    myFunction(3, 1, 2, 3);
    return 0;
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <cstdarg>
#include <cstdio>

void myFunction(int count, ...){
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, count);
    for (int i = 0; i < count; i++) { // Correct: handle based on actual number of parameters
        int value = va_arg(args, int);
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    va_end(args);
}

int main() {
    myFunction(3, 1, 2, 3); // Correct call
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
此规则检测在使用可变参数宏 `va_start` 时，传入的参数是否正确。

######## 规则意义
错误使用 `va_start` 可能导致未定义行为，包括访问内存错误和程序崩溃。确保在调用 `va_start` 之前，传入的参数是正确的类型。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <cstdarg>
#include <cstdio>

void example(int count, ...)
{
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, count); // 错误: 需要传递最后一个命名参数
    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        int value = va_arg(args, int);
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    va_end(args);
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <cstdarg>
#include <cstdio>

void example(int first, int count, ...)
{
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, count); // 正确: 传递了最后一个命名参数
    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        int value = va_arg(args, int);
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    va_end(args);
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule detects whether the parameters passed when using the variadic macro `va_start` are correct.

######## Rule Meaning
Incorrect use of `va_start` may lead to undefined behavior, including memory access errors and program crashes. It is essential to ensure that the parameters passed to `va_start` are of the correct type before calling it.

######## Error Example
```cpp
#include <cstdarg>
#include <cstdio>

void example(int count, ...)
{
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, count); // Error: The last named parameter needs to be passed
    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        int value = va_arg(args, int);
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    va_end(args);
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <cstdarg>
#include <cstdio>

void example(int first, int count, ...)
{
    va_list args;
    va_start(args, count); // Correct: The last named parameter is passed
    for (int i = 0; i < count; ++i) {
        int value = va_arg(args, int);
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    va_end(args);
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在使用变量作为函数返回值或参数时，如果变量可能为空(NULL)，则应进行空值检查。

######## 规则意义
不安全的空值访问可能导致运行时错误，例如崩溃或未定义行为。这条规则旨在确保对变量的空值检查，减少潜在的缺陷。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <iostream>

void process(int* ptr) {
    std::cout << *ptr << std::endl; // 可能导致空指针解引用
}

int main() {
    int* nullPtr = nullptr;
    process(nullPtr); // 错误: 传递空指针
    return 0;
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <iostream>

void process(int* ptr) {
    if (ptr != nullptr) {
        std::cout << *ptr << std::endl; // 安全访问
    } else {
        std::cout << "指针为空！" << std::endl;
    }
}

int main() {
    int* nullPtr = nullptr;
    process(nullPtr); // 安全处理
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
When using variables as function return values or parameters, a null check should be performed if the variable could be null (NULL).

######## Rule Meaning
Unsafe null access may lead to runtime errors, such as crashes or undefined behavior. This rule aims to ensure that null checks are performed on variables, reducing potential defects.

######## Error Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>

void process(int* ptr) {
    std::cout << *ptr << std::endl; // May lead to null pointer dereference
}

int main() {
    int* nullPtr = nullptr;
    process(nullPtr); // Error: passing a null pointer
    return 0;
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <iostream>

void process(int* ptr) {
    if (ptr != nullptr) {
        std::cout << *ptr << std::endl; // Safe access
    } else {
        std::cout << "Pointer is null!" << std::endl;
    }
}

int main() {
    int* nullPtr = nullptr;
    process(nullPtr); // Safe handling
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
此规则检查变量的作用域，以确保变量的可见性和生命周期是合理的，避免不必要的作用域扩展。

######## 规则意义
合理的变量作用域可以降低代码的复杂性，减少潜在的错误，并提高代码的可维护性。过大的作用域可能导致变量被意外修改或冲突，而过小的作用域则可能影响性能。

######## 错误示例
'''c++
void example() {
    int a = 10;
    if (true) {
        int b = 20;
    }
    // 在这里访问 b 会导致错误
    // std::cout << b; // 错误：b 在此作用域未定义
}
'''

######## 正确示例
'''c++
void example() {
    int a = 10;
    int b = 20; // b 的作用域扩大到整个函数
    std::cout << b; // 正确：b 在此作用域可访问
}
'''

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule checks the scope of variables to ensure that the visibility and lifecycle of variables are reasonable, avoiding unnecessary scope expansion.

######## Significance of the Rule
Appropriate variable scope can reduce code complexity, minimize potential errors, and improve code maintainability. An overly large scope may lead to unintended modifications or conflicts with variables, whereas a too-small scope may impact performance.

######## Error Example
'''c++
void example() {
    int a = 10;
    if (true) {
        int b = 20;
    }
    // Accessing b here will cause an error
    // std::cout << b; // Error: b is not defined in this scope
}
'''

######## Correct Example
'''c++
void example() {
    int a = 10;
    int b = 20; // The scope of b is expanded to the entire function
    std::cout << b; // Correct: b is accessible in this scope
}
'''

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
在构造函数中调用虚函数会导致未定义行为，因为对象尚未完全构造，虚函数可能会调用基类或子类中的不同实现。

######## 规则意义
调用虚函数时，实际对象的类型可能不是预期的那个类型。尤其在构造阶段，基类的构造函数执行完成前，虚表指针指向的可能还是基类，调用子类的虚函数可能会导致非预期的行为或者程序崩溃。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    Base() { doSomething(); } // 错误：在构造函数中调用虚函数
    virtual void doSomething() { /* 基类实现 */ }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    virtual void doSomething() override { /* 子类实现 */ }
};
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    virtual void initialize() { /* 初始化基类 */ }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void setup() {
        initialize(); // 正确：在对象完全构造后调用
    }
};
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
Calling virtual functions in a constructor leads to undefined behavior because the object is not yet fully constructed, and the virtual function may call different implementations in the base class or derived class.

######## Rule Significance
When calling a virtual function, the actual type of the object may not be the expected type. Especially during the construction phase, before the base class constructor completes, the virtual table pointer may still point to the base class, and calling a derived class's virtual function may result in unexpected behavior or program crashes.

######## Error Example
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    Base() { doSomething(); } // Error: Calling a virtual function in the constructor
    virtual void doSomething() { /* Base class implementation */ }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    virtual void doSomething() override { /* Derived class implementation */ }
};
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    virtual void initialize() { /* Initialize base class */ }
};

class Derived : public Base {
public:
    void setup() {
        initialize(); // Correct: Calling after object is fully constructed
    }
};
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
当类使用虚函数时，如果该类有析构函数，应该将其声明为虚拟析构函数。

######## 规则意义
不将析构函数指定为虚拟的将导致在删除基类指针的派生类对象时，基类析构函数被调用，而派生类析构函数不会被执行。这会导致资源泄露和未定义行为。确保基类的析构函数是虚拟的，能保证派生类的析构函数在对象被删除时被正确调用。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    ~Base() {} // 非虚拟析构函数
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    ~Derived() {}
};

void func() {
    Base* b = new Derived();
    delete b; // 只会调用Base的析构函数，Derived析构函数未被调用
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    virtual ~Base() {} // 虚拟析构函数
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    ~Derived() {}
};

void func() {
    Base* b = new Derived();
    delete b; // 将正确调用Derived的析构函数
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
When a class uses virtual functions, if that class has a destructor, it should be declared as a virtual destructor.

######## Rule Meaning
Failing to specify the destructor as virtual will lead to the base class destructor being called when deleting a derived class object through a base class pointer, while the derived class destructor will not be executed. This can result in resource leaks and undefined behavior. Ensuring that the base class destructor is virtual guarantees that the derived class destructor is called correctly when the object is deleted.

######## Error Example
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    ~Base() {} // Non-virtual destructor
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    ~Derived() {}
};

void func() {
    Base* b = new Derived();
    delete b; // Only the Base destructor is called, the Derived destructor is not called
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
class Base {
public:
    virtual ~Base() {} // Virtual destructor
};
class Derived : public Base {
public:
    ~Derived() {}
};

void func() {
    Base* b = new Derived();
    delete b; // Correctly calls the Derived destructor
}
```

rules.content:
####### 规则概述
此规则检测代码中尝试向只读文件写入内容的情况。

######## 规则意义
试图修改只读文件会导致程序运行时出错，通常会引发IO异常或程序崩溃。确保在写入文件之前验证文件的写入权限是良好的编程实践。

######## 错误示例
```cpp
#include <fstream>

int main() {
    const char* filename = "readonly.txt";
    std::ofstream ofs(filename);
    ofs << "Writing to a read-only file."; // 错误: 向只读文件写入
    ofs.close();
    return 0;
}
```

######## 正确示例
```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <sys/stat.h>

bool isFileWritable(const char* filename) {
    struct stat buffer;
    return (stat(filename, &buffer) == 0) && (buffer.st_mode & S_IWUSR);
}

int main() {
    const char* filename = "readonly.txt";
    if (isFileWritable(filename)) {
        std::ofstream ofs(filename);
        ofs << "Writing to the file.";
        ofs.close();
    } else {
        std::cerr << "Error: File is read-only, cannot write.";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

rules.content_en:
####### Rule Overview
This rule detects instances in code where an attempt is made to write to a read-only file.

######## Rule Meaning
Attempting to modify a read-only file can lead to runtime errors in the program, typically resulting in an IO exception or program crash. It is good programming practice to ensure that file write permissions are verified before writing to a file.

######## Error Example
```cpp
#include <fstream>

int main() {
    const char* filename = "readonly.txt";
    std::ofstream ofs(filename);
    ofs << "Writing to a read-only file."; // Error: Writing to a read-only file
    ofs.close();
    return 0;
}
```

######## Correct Example
```cpp
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <sys/stat.h>

bool isFileWritable(const char* filename) {
    struct stat buffer;
    return (stat(filename, &buffer) == 0) && (buffer.st_mode & S_IWUSR);
}

int main() {
    const char* filename = "readonly.txt";
    if (isFileWritable(filename)) {
        std::ofstream ofs(filename);
        ofs << "Writing to the file.";
        ofs.close();
    } else {
        std::cerr << "Error: File is read-only, cannot write.";
    }
    return 0;
}
```

